I added the opt-in fence and committed it as fcecb67a ("Part of #879"). I also posted what's still open as a comment on #879. Build, all tests, clippy -D warnings and fmt --check pass.

What it does: when YOYO_ASK_OUTSIDE_READS=1 is set, read_file asks before reading a path that resolves (symlinks followed) to somewhere outside the working directory.
• In a terminal: you get a y/n prompt. A yes covers that file's folder for the rest of the session, but not its subfolders, so saying yes to your home folder doesn't also open ~/.ssh. A no refuses just that read, and the next read in that folder asks again.
• With no terminal (-p in a pipe, CI): the read is refused. The model gets a message naming the path and saying how to allow it. The message is marked as a deliberate refusal, so yoyo won't treat it as a flaky tool and suggest retrying.
• Not asked: paths that don't exist (read_file reports its own error), and any read when --allow-dir is set, since that list is already your answer.
• Env var unset, or set to anything but `1`: nothing changes from today. That's every user who hasn't opted in.

Files:
• `src/outside_read.rs` (new): all the logic and 8 tests. The decision takes its inputs (enabled, has-a-terminal, the answer) as parameters, so tests never touch the real environment or stdin.
• `src/tool_wrappers.rs`: one call in each of the two path-guard wrappers, right after the existing directory check. I also made is_deterministic_refusal visible to the new module so a test can confirm the refusals are recognised.
• Other edits: a mod line in src/main.rs, a new subsection in docs/src/configuration/permissions.md, and the size register in tests/module_size.rs updated from 5622 to 5684.

Checks:
• Positive control: with the enabled check removed, exactly one test failed (disabled_other_tools_and_allow_dir_are_untouched). With the file restored, all 8 passed again.
• Lock gate: the repo's lock-recovery check failed my first draft because I'd written my own lock-recovery line. I switched it to sync_util::lock_or_recover.
• Not run: I didn't try the fence end to end in a real session, because that needs a live model. It's covered only by the unit tests and the wiring.

I chose two things the task didn't specify: that --allow-dir counts as consent, and that a yes doesn't cover subfolders. Both are stated in the docs and the module comment.

Still open on #879:
• search and list_files aren't covered yet.
• There's no CLI flag or .yoyo.toml key, only the env var.
• Whether to ask by default is still undecided. It stays off on purpose for now, because asking by default breaks piped and CI runs (#448).
• It's a fence on one file tool, not a sandbox: bash can still cat the same file.
